y in her NEXT class. It MUST be rooted in THIS exact lesson AND fuse her own value (from her Q3 answer + strengths) with THE TARGET below. Phrase it as an implementation intention anchored to next class (\"Next class, when [trigger], [do X]\") — but respect the gender-neutral rule above (imperative, not a gendered \"you will\"). Max ~32 words. Vivid and classroom-specific — name the actual materials/concept from THIS lesson. NOT generic.\n\nAlso return \"highlights\": an array of 2–4 short English keyword phrases that appear verbatim in \"action\" (concrete nouns) to visually emphasise. And \"lesson_label\": a 2–4 word English subject·topic label.\n\nSession (framework: FICO):\nTHE TARGET (fixed — do NOT choose a different area): indicator B6 \"Differentiation / Catering to Learning Levels\". Why it is the next step: All learners were given identical tasks (\"آپ نے اس کا فیکشن لکھنا ہے\"), so a small change (two levels of task) would let all students practice at the right challenge and increase learning gains quickly. The scorer's suggested move: \"Prepare one easier counting version and one extension question; tell half the class to do the easier task and faster students to do the extension.\". Your \"action\" is about THIS indicator only — ONE move, not a list of moves.\n\n- Her strengths: Clear objective with concrete task\n- Growth areas: Differentiation (B6) — All students received the same whole-class tasks and the teacher assigned the same writing task to everyone: \"آپ نے اس کا فیکشن لکھنا ہے۔\" (\"You have to write its fraction.\"). Strategy: Prepare two versions of the practice: a simpler counting task (identify shaded parts) and a stretch task (compare two fractions or create a non-proper fraction). || Problem-Solving & Productive Struggle (F5) — Lesson presents routine identification and writing tasks; no non-routine or multi-step problems are posed, and teacher gives or models answers quickly with little think time. Strategy: Introduce one multi-step or non-routine task (e.g. 'Show two different ways to make 1/2 using any shapes') and give 90–120 seconds silent think time before asking for answers.\n- Q3 question we asked her: In the opening minutes, a student defined a fraction as 'above 3, below 4,' focusing on notation. Later, when you showed a …(+558)"}],"response_format":{"type":"json_object"}},"value":{"id":"gen-1794291086-Sxntqpbs0wrIMXYeYTSd","object":"chat.completion","created":1794291086,"model":"openai/gpt-5-mini","provider":"OpenAI","system_fingerprint":null,"service_tier":"default","choices":[{"index":0,"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop","native_finish_reason":"completed","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"commitment\":\"You’ll keep the steady pacing and give quieter students more think time to answer.\",\"action\":\"Next class, when handing out the write its fraction worksheet, give quieter students the counting version (identify shaded parts, write numerator/denominator) and faster students the extension (compare two fractions).\",\"lesson_label\":\"Math Fractions\",\"highlights\":[\"write its fraction worksheet\",\"counting version\",\"quieter students\",\"compare two fractions\"]}","refusal":null,"reasoning":"**Formulating JSON for lesson plan**\n\nI need to create a JSON format that reflects the teacher's values and strengths, focusing on pacing and providing quieter students with additional time. I’ll include specific actions related to the next class, emphasizing concrete tasks for differentiation. The actions should connect to triggers like handing out writing tasks or explaining the fraction model, ensuring they're vivid and relevant to this lesson's materials like fractions and shaded shapes. I’ll aim for clarity and detail throughout.**Planning concrete actions for the lesson**\n\nI’m looking to create a concise instruction for next class involving the \"write its fraction\" task with shaded shapes. I'll split the students based on their skill levels: half will do the counting 